      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Francesco Redi and Louis Pasteur conducted controlled experiments to test this hypothesis.",
      "evidence": "Redi used covered jars to prevent fly access; Pasteur used swan-neck flasks to prevent airborne contamination.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "These experiments demonstrated that microorganisms and insects come from pre-existing life, not from the non-living matter itself.",
      "evidence": "Pasteur's broth remained sterile in sealed/swan-neck flasks but grew microbes when the neck was broken; Redi's covered meat had no maggots.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [
        2
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The core methodology used to disprove spontaneous generation was controlled experimentation.",
      "evidence": "The scientific method relies on empirical testing and control variables to establish causality, which is what Redi and Pasteur employed.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [
        3
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "While microscopes aided observation, the disproof relied on the experimental design (experimentation) rather than just the tool.",
      "evidence": "Redi's experiment did not require a microscope to show flies didn't appear in covered meat; the logical refutation came from the control conditions.",
